#!/usr/bin/bash
# ==============================================================================
# aquarius-resolve-install — set up DaVinci Resolve on AquariusOS
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# DaVinci Resolve is a professional video editor made by Blackmagic Design.
# Their licence does not let anyone else hand out the installer, so AquariusOS
# cannot include it. What AquariusOS CAN do is everything around it — and that
# is a great deal more than it sounds, because Resolve on Linux is famously
# awkward and this makes it not be.
#
# WHAT IT DOES, IN ORDER
#   1. Explains itself.
#   2. Works out how this computer's graphics card can be reached from inside a
#      container, and says so plainly if it cannot be.
#   3. Finds Blackmagic's download — or opens their page and WAITS for it.
#   4. Downloads a Rocky Linux runtime (about a gigabyte, once) and makes a
#      container from it.
#   5. Runs Blackmagic's installer inside that container.
#   6. Puts "DaVinci Resolve" in your apps.
#
# Nothing about the operating system is changed. Everything lives in one
# container that `aq resolve remove` deletes without trace.
#
# THE TWO RULES OF THIS SCRIPT'S VOICE
#   1. Say what is about to happen before it happens.
#   2. Never end on an error without saying what to do next.
#
# The beginner-facing guide is docs/restart/resolve.md.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE PROGRESS CHANNEL — how the installer window follows along
# ==============================================================================
# THIS SCRIPT IS THE ONLY THING THAT INSTALLS RESOLVE. The window a person
# clicks in their apps (/usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-installer) does not
# reimplement any of it — it RUNS this script and reads what it says. That is
# deliberate and it is the rule: one installer, two faces.
#
# Everything you can read below still goes to stdout and stderr, exactly as it
# always did, so `aq resolve install` in a terminal looks the same as it ever
# looked. The window needs something a machine can read as well, so it opens a
# spare pipe, tells us its number, and we write short structured lines to it and
# to nothing else.
#
#     aquarius-resolve-install --progress-fd 5      (or AQ_PROGRESS_FD=5)
#
# THE LINES. This list is the contract. The same list is repeated at the top of
# aquarius-resolve-installer, and if you change one you change both.
#
#   STEP <n>/<total> <text>
#       Step <n> of <total> has just begun. <text> is a plain-English name for
#       it and the window shows exactly that text, so the wording lives HERE and
#       nowhere else. Beginning a step means the ones before it finished.
#
#   PERCENT <0-100>
#       How far through the CURRENT step we are. Only sent where there is a real
#       number to send — at the moment that means the container download, whose
#       size podman tells us. A step with no PERCENT is not a stalled step; the
#       window shows a moving bar with no end for it, which is the honest
#       picture.
#
#   DONE
#       Every step finished. Sent once, last.
#
#   FAIL <text>
#       Stopped, and <text> says why in words a person can act on.
#
# Nothing else is ever written to that pipe. Anything unrecognised is ignored by
# the window rather than shown, so adding a line here cannot break an older one.
#
# THE STEPS, IN ORDER (the window draws this list before anything starts, so
# keep it and the STEP lines below in step with each other):
#
#     1  Checking your graphics card
#     2  Finding your download
#     3  Downloading the environment Resolve runs in
#     4  Setting that environment up
#     5  Installing DaVinci Resolve
#     6  Adding it to your apps
#
# ⚠️ NOTE THE WORDING. None of the six says "Rocky Linux" any more, and that is
# Royce's rule from the bench test on 2026-09-04: a person installing a video
# editor is not helped by being told which Linux is inside the box. The comments
# in this file still say Rocky, because whoever maintains it does need to know.
# Everything a USER reads says "environment". The same rule applies to the
# window, to notifications, and to the .desktop files.
#
# REMOVING walks three steps on the same channel, so one window can read either:
#
#     1  Removing DaVinci Resolve from your apps
#     2  Deleting the environment it ran in
#     3  Keeping your projects and settings  (or deleting them, with --purge)
#
# TWO QUESTIONS THE WINDOW ASKS BEFORE IT STARTS ANYTHING, so that it is never
# guessing where this script would look or what this script would find:
#
#     aquarius-resolve-install --find-installer
#         Prints the download it would use, or prints nothing and exits 1.
#
#     aquarius-resolve-install --gpu-summary
#         Prints one line: a word (ok / warn / none), a tab, then a sentence.
#
# AND A REHEARSAL:
#
#     aquarius-resolve-install --dry-run
#         Walks all six steps, changing nothing at all. It is how the window
#         gets looked at without reinstalling Resolve, and it is what CI runs to
#         prove the lines above still arrive in the right order.
# ==============================================================================

set -uo pipefail

# Every person who runs this has a HOME. A build container checking the script
# with --dry-run may not, and `set -u` would stop it dead on the next line with
# an unbound-variable message that says nothing about what is wrong.
: "${HOME:=/tmp}"

RUNTIME_ENV=/usr/share/aquarius/resolve/runtime.env
RECORD_DIR="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-${HOME}/.local/share}/aquarius/resolve"
RECORD="${RECORD_DIR}/installed.env"
DOWNLOADS="${HOME}/Downloads"

# ==============================================================================
# What was asked for
# ==============================================================================
MODE="install"
INPUT=""
QUERY=""
DRY_RUN="${AQ_RESOLVE_DRY_RUN:-0}"
PROGRESS_FD="${AQ_PROGRESS_FD:-}"
# Removing: --purge also deletes the person's own Resolve settings and project
# database. It is off unless asked for, every single time, and the two windows
# that can ask for it both default it to off.
PURGE=0
ASSUME_YES=0

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --update) MODE="update" ;;
        --remove | --uninstall) MODE="remove" ;;
        --purge) PURGE=1 ;;
        --yes | -y) ASSUME_YES=1 ;;
        --dry-run) DRY_RUN=1 ;;
        --gpu-summary) QUERY="gpu" ;;
        --find-installer) QUERY="find" ;;
        --progress-fd)
            shift
            PROGRESS_FD="${1:-}"
            ;;
        --progress-fd=*) PROGRESS_FD="${1#*=}" ;;
        -*)
            echo "aquarius-resolve: I do not understand '${1}'." >&2
            exit 1
            ;;
        *) INPUT="$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
done

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The progress channel
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A file-descriptor number and nothing else. Anything that is not a plain number
# is treated as "no channel", because the alternative is every later write
# printing a shell error into the middle of the person's log.
case "${PROGRESS_FD}" in
    '' | *[!0-9]*) PROGRESS_FD="" ;;
esac

# Copy it to a fixed number — 9 — and use only that from here on.
#
# WHY BOTHER: writing to a descriptor whose number is in a variable is legal but
# it reads as a mistake to both people and checking tools, and it has to be
# re-checked at every single write. Copying once means every write below is the
# plain, obvious `>&9`. If the copy fails — a number naming a pipe that was
# closed behind us is a real possibility — we fall silent, which is much better
# than spraying "Bad file descriptor" through somebody's log.
PROGRESS_OPEN=0
if [ -n "${PROGRESS_FD}" ]; then
    if eval "exec 9>&${PROGRESS_FD}" 2> /dev/null; then
        PROGRESS_OPEN=1
    fi
fi

# Installing walks six steps; removing walks three. Both use the same channel
# and the same words for it, which is what lets one window read either.
STEP_TOTAL=6
[ "${MODE}" = "remove" ] && STEP_TOTAL=3

progress() {
    [ "${PROGRESS_OPEN}" = "1" ] || return 0
    printf '%s\n' "$*" >&9 || true
}
step() { progress "STEP ${1}/${STEP_TOTAL} ${2}"; }
pct() { progress "PERCENT ${1}"; }
# Used on every path that leaves this script unhappily. The window shows this
# sentence in large type, so it has to be a sentence, not a code.
fail() { progress "FAIL ${*}"; }

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which runtime container, and what it is called on this machine
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The answer normally comes from one file in the image. It can be overridden by
# setting the same names in the environment first, which exists for exactly one
# purpose: TESTING A RUNTIME THAT HAS NOT BEEN RELEASED YET on the bench.
#
#     AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_TAG=dev-restart-r3-resolve aq resolve install
#
# Side branches publish under a `dev-` tag so that a work-in-progress runtime
# can be tried on real hardware without ever touching the `9` tag that finished
# machines pull. Nobody needs this in normal use, and it is deliberately not
# mentioned in the help text.
OVERRIDE_IMAGE="${AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_IMAGE:-}"
OVERRIDE_TAG="${AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_TAG:-}"

IMAGE=""
CONTAINER="aquarius-resolve"

# The two question flags and the rehearsal answer without touching anything, so
# none of them needs this file to be readable. Everything else does.
if [ -z "${QUERY}" ] && [ "${DRY_RUN}" != "1" ]; then
    if [ ! -r "${RUNTIME_ENV}" ]; then
        fail "This copy of AquariusOS is missing its Resolve support files. That is a fault in the operating system, not in anything you did."
        echo "aquarius-resolve: ${RUNTIME_ENV} is missing from this system." >&2
        echo "  That is a fault in AquariusOS, not in anything you typed." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "${RUNTIME_ENV}"

    [ -n "${OVERRIDE_IMAGE}" ] && AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_IMAGE="${OVERRIDE_IMAGE}"
    [ -n "${OVERRIDE_TAG}" ] && AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_TAG="${OVERRIDE_TAG}"

    IMAGE="${AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_IMAGE}:${AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_TAG}"
    CONTAINER="${AQ_RESOLVE_CONTAINER}"

    if [ -n "${OVERRIDE_IMAGE}${OVERRIDE_TAG}" ]; then
        echo "NOTE: using a runtime named in the environment, not the one this"
        echo "      version of AquariusOS ships with:  ${IMAGE}"
    fi
fi

# ==============================================================================
# How this computer is looked at
# ==============================================================================
# These four functions are the ONLY place AquariusOS decides what graphics card
# is in a machine and which file is the Blackmagic download. The install below
# uses them, and so do `--gpu-summary` and `--find-installer`, which is how the
# window gets the same answer as the terminal instead of a second opinion.
# ==============================================================================

have_nvidia() {
    [ -e /proc/driver/nvidia/version ] && return 0
    [ -c /dev/nvidiactl ] && return 0
    return 1
}

nvidia_driver_version() {
    sed -n 's/^NVRM version: NVIDIA UNIX.* Kernel Module *\([0-9.]*\).*/\1/p' \
        /proc/driver/nvidia/version 2> /dev/null | head -1
}

# The card's own name, for the one line a person reads before they commit to
# fifteen minutes. nvidia-smi is the reliable answer and is part of the driver;
# the /proc file is there for the moments it is not on the path.
nvidia_model() {
    local m
    m="$(nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name --format=csv,noheader 2> /dev/null | head -1)"
    if [ -z "${m}" ]; then
        m="$(sed -n 's/^Model: *//p' /proc/driver/nvidia/gpus/*/information 2> /dev/null | head -1)"
    fi
    printf '%s' "${m}"
}

find_cdi_spec() {
    local f
    # /run/cdi and /var/run/cdi are the same folder on any modern Linux;
    # both names are checked because both appear in NVIDIA's own documentation.
    for f in /var/run/cdi/nvidia.yaml /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml \
        /etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml /etc/cdi/nvidia.json; do
        if [ -r "${f}" ]; then
            printf '%s' "${f}"
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# One line: a word, a tab, a sentence. The word is how the window chooses
# between a tick and a warning triangle; the sentence is what it prints.
gpu_summary() {
    local model version major
    if have_nvidia; then
        model="$(nvidia_model)"
        [ -n "${model}" ] || model="An NVIDIA graphics card"
        version="$(nvidia_driver_version)"
        major="${version%%.*}"
        if [ -n "${version}" ] && [ "${major:-0}" -lt 580 ] 2> /dev/null; then
            printf 'warn\t%s, driver %s — DaVinci Resolve 21 expects driver 580 or newer. Update with: sudo bootc upgrade\n' \
                "${model}" "${version}"
        else
            printf 'ok\t%s — CUDA ready\n' "${model}"
        fi
        return 0
    fi
    if [ -e /dev/dri ]; then
        printf 'warn\t%s\n' "AMD or Intel graphics. Blackmagic's only supported Linux graphics is NVIDIA, so Resolve will most likely say its GPU processing mode is unsupported."
        return 0
    fi
    printf 'none\t%s\n' "No graphics card could be found. Resolve will install, but it will not be able to do much."
}

# Newest match wins — that is what "I just downloaded it" means. A .run is
# preferred over a .zip because it is already unpacked.
find_one() { # find_one <pattern>
    {
        find "${DOWNLOADS}" -maxdepth 2 -type f -iname "$1" 2> /dev/null
        find "${HOME}" -maxdepth 1 -type f -iname "$1" 2> /dev/null
    } | sort -u | while IFS= read -r f; do
        printf '%s\t%s\n' "$(stat -c '%Y' "${f}" 2> /dev/null || echo 0)" "${f}"
    done | sort -rn | head -1 | cut -f2-
}

find_installer() {
    local found
    found="$(find_one 'DaVinci_Resolve*_Linux.run')"
    [ -n "${found}" ] && {
        printf '%s' "${found}"
        return 0
    }
    found="$(find_one 'DaVinci_Resolve*Linux*.zip')"
    [ -n "${found}" ] && {
        printf '%s' "${found}"
        return 0
    }
    return 1
}

# ==============================================================================
# The two questions, answered and gone
# ==============================================================================
case "${QUERY}" in
    gpu)
        gpu_summary
        exit 0
        ;;
    find)
        if found="$(find_installer)"; then
            printf '%s\n' "${found}"
            exit 0
        fi
        exit 1
        ;;
esac

# ==============================================================================
# REMOVING IT AGAIN
# ==============================================================================
# `aq resolve remove`, and the "Remove DaVinci Resolve" window, both end up
# here. It lives in this script rather than in `aq` for the same reason the
# installing does: ONE PLACE OWNS THE CONTAINER, and a window and a terminal
# that do the same job must not be two implementations of it.
#
# THE THING THIS HAS TO GET RIGHT, ABOVE EVERYTHING ELSE:
#
#   YOUR PROJECTS AND YOUR MEDIA ARE NOT IN THE CONTAINER. They are in your
#   ordinary folders, which the container only borrows while it is running. So
#   deleting the container deletes Resolve and nothing of yours.
#
#   The one exception is Resolve's own database — the project library, its
#   settings, your keyboard customisations — which Resolve keeps in
#   ~/.local/share/DaVinciResolve. That IS yours, it is small, and it is what
#   makes a reinstall feel like the same computer. So it is KEPT unless somebody
#   deliberately asks for it to go, with --purge, which both windows leave
#   unticked.
#
# Three steps, on the same progress channel as installing, so one window can
# read either.
if [ "${MODE}" = "remove" ]; then
    RESOLVE_DB="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-${HOME}/.local/share}/DaVinciResolve"
    APPS_DIR="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-${HOME}/.local/share}/applications"

    echo ""
    echo "=================================================================="
    if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
        echo " A REHEARSAL — nothing is being removed"
    else
        echo " Removing DaVinci Resolve from AquariusOS"
    fi
    echo "=================================================================="
    echo ""
    echo "This removes DaVinci Resolve and the separate environment it ran in."
    echo ""
    echo "YOUR PROJECTS AND MEDIA ARE NOT IN THERE. They are in your ordinary"
    echo "folders, which that environment only borrowed while it was running."
    echo "Nothing you have made is touched by this."
    echo ""
    if [ "${PURGE}" = "1" ]; then
        echo "You have also asked for your Resolve settings and project database"
        echo "to be deleted:"
        echo "    ${RESOLVE_DB}"
        echo ""
    else
        echo "Your Resolve settings and project database are being KEPT, so that"
        echo "installing it again picks up where you left off."
        echo ""
    fi

    # Nothing exists and nothing is asked. Said plainly rather than treated as
    # a failure — "there was nothing to remove" is a fine outcome.
    if [ "${DRY_RUN}" != "1" ] \
        && ! podman container exists "${CONTAINER}" 2> /dev/null; then
        echo "There is nothing to remove — DaVinci Resolve is not set up on this"
        echo "computer."
        if [ "${PURGE}" = "1" ] && [ -d "${RESOLVE_DB}" ]; then
            echo "Your Resolve settings folder is still here, and you asked for it"
            echo "to go, so that is being done."
        else
            progress "STEP 1/3 Nothing to remove"
            progress "DONE"
            exit 0
        fi
    fi

    # Ask, unless somebody already answered. The windows always pass --yes,
    # because they asked in a dialog before they got here.
    if [ "${ASSUME_YES}" != "1" ] && [ "${DRY_RUN}" != "1" ]; then
        read -r -p "Remove it? [y/N] " reply
        case "${reply}" in
            [yY]*) ;;
            *)
                echo "Nothing was changed."
                exit 0
                ;;
        esac
        echo ""
    fi

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 1. The menu entries go FIRST
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # An icon left behind that starts something which no longer exists is worse
    # than no icon: it is a computer that lies to you. So they go before the
    # thing they point at, not after.
    step 1 "Removing DaVinci Resolve from your apps"
    removed=0
    if [ -d "${APPS_DIR}" ]; then
        while IFS= read -r entry; do
            [ -n "${entry}" ] || continue
            # Only entries that point at OUR launcher. Somebody who also has
            # Resolve installed another way keeps their own.
            grep -q 'aquarius-resolve-launch\|aquarius-resolve-run' "${entry}" || continue
            if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
                echo "  would remove ${entry}"
                removed=$((removed + 1))
            elif rm -f "${entry}"; then
                removed=$((removed + 1))
            fi
        done < <(find "${APPS_DIR}" -maxdepth 1 -type f \
            \( -iname '*resolve*.desktop' \
            -o -iname '*davinci*.desktop' \
            -o -iname '*blackmagic*.desktop' \) 2> /dev/null | sort)
    fi
    echo "  ${removed} menu entries removed."
    [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ] || update-desktop-database "${APPS_DIR}" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 2. The environment itself
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    step 2 "Deleting the environment it ran in"
    if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
        echo "  would delete the '${CONTAINER}' container."
    elif podman container exists "${CONTAINER}" 2> /dev/null; then
        echo "  Deleting '${CONTAINER}'. This takes a moment."
        if ! distrobox rm --force "${CONTAINER}"; then
            fail "The environment could not be deleted. Nothing else was changed. Try again, or from a terminal: distrobox rm --force ${CONTAINER}"
            echo "" >&2
            echo "It could not be deleted." >&2
            echo "From a terminal:  distrobox rm --force ${CONTAINER}" >&2
            exit 1
        fi
        rm -f "${RECORD}"
        echo "  Gone."
    else
        echo "  There was no environment to delete."
    fi

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 3. Your own settings — kept, unless you said otherwise
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if [ "${PURGE}" = "1" ]; then
        step 3 "Deleting your Resolve settings and project database"
        if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
            echo "  would delete ${RESOLVE_DB}"
        elif [ -d "${RESOLVE_DB}" ]; then
            if rm -rf "${RESOLVE_DB}"; then
                echo "  Deleted ${RESOLVE_DB}"
            else
                fail "Resolve was removed, but your settings folder could not be deleted. Nothing else is wrong."
                echo "Could not delete ${RESOLVE_DB}" >&2
                exit 1
            fi
        else
            echo "  There was no settings folder to delete."
        fi
    else
        step 3 "Keeping your projects and settings"
        echo "  Your Resolve settings and project database are still here:"
        echo "    ${RESOLVE_DB}"
        echo "  Install Resolve again and it picks up where you left off."
    fi

    progress "DONE"
    echo ""
    echo "=================================================================="
    if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
        echo " Rehearsal finished. Nothing was removed and nothing was changed."
    else
        echo " Done. DaVinci Resolve has been removed."
    fi
    echo "=================================================================="
    echo ""
    echo "The downloaded environment image is still on this computer, so setting"
    echo "Resolve up again is quick. To reclaim that space as well:"
    echo "    podman image prune -a"
    echo ""
    exit 0
fi

# ==============================================================================
# The rehearsal
# ==============================================================================
# --dry-run walks the same six steps and changes nothing whatsoever. It exists
# for two reasons: so the installer window can be looked at and adjusted without
# reinstalling Resolve, and so CI can prove the structured lines above still
# come out in the right order on every build. It touches no container, no
# download, and no file.
if [ "${DRY_RUN}" = "1" ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "=================================================================="
    echo " A REHEARSAL — nothing is being installed"
    echo "=================================================================="
    echo ""
    echo "This walks through the same six steps a real setup goes through, so"
    echo "that the window can be looked at, without touching DaVinci Resolve,"
    echo "your container, or anything you have downloaded."
    echo ""
    step 1 "Checking your graphics card"
    gpu_summary | sed 's/^[a-z]*\t/  /'
    sleep 0.3
    step 2 "Finding your download"
    echo "  Pretending a download was found in ${DOWNLOADS}."
    sleep 0.3
    step 3 "Downloading the environment Resolve runs in"
    for p in 0 25 50 75 100; do
        pct "${p}"
        echo "  ...pretending to download: ${p}%"
        sleep 0.2
    done
    step 4 "Setting that environment up"
    echo "  Pretending to create the container."
    sleep 0.3
    step 5 "Installing DaVinci Resolve"
    echo "  Pretending to run Blackmagic's installer."
    sleep 0.3
    step 6 "Adding it to your apps"
    echo "  Pretending to write the app-menu entry."
    sleep 0.2
    progress "DONE"
    echo ""
    echo "Rehearsal finished. Nothing was installed and nothing was changed."
    echo "The real thing is:   aq resolve install"
    echo ""
    exit 0
fi

# ==============================================================================
# 0. What is about to happen
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
echo "=================================================================="
if [ "${MODE}" = "update" ]; then
    echo " Updating DaVinci Resolve on AquariusOS"
else
    echo " Setting up DaVinci Resolve on AquariusOS"
fi
echo "=================================================================="
echo ""
echo "DaVinci Resolve is made by Blackmagic Design. Their licence does not"
echo "allow anyone else to hand out the installer, so you download it from"
echo "them and this does the rest."
echo ""
echo "Resolve runs inside its own protected environment on this computer, so"
echo "it stays stable across updates and cannot clash with anything else you"
echo "have installed. That is the single most common reason people give up on"
echo "Resolve on Linux, and it is why this exists."
echo ""
echo "You will not notice the environment. Resolve appears in your apps like"
echo "any other program and reads and writes your ordinary folders."
echo ""
echo "This takes roughly fifteen minutes, nearly all of it downloading."
echo "Stop at any time with Ctrl+C — nothing will be left broken."
echo ""

# ==============================================================================
# 1. Is the machinery here?
# ==============================================================================
for tool in podman distrobox; do
    if ! command -v "${tool}" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        fail "This computer is missing '${tool}', which the setup needs. That should not be possible on AquariusOS — please report it."
        echo "This needs '${tool}', which is missing from this system." >&2
        echo "That should not be possible on AquariusOS — please report it." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
done

# ==============================================================================
# 2. How does the graphics card get into the container?
# ==============================================================================
# THIS SECTION IS THE HEART OF THE FEATURE. Without a graphics card Resolve
# starts and then says its GPU processing mode is unsupported, which is the
# single most common complaint about Resolve on Linux.
#
# THERE ARE TWO WAYS OF DOING IT AND WE PREFER THE FIRST:
#
# (a) CDI — the "Container Device Interface". NVIDIA's own, supported mechanism.
#     A file on this machine describes the graphics card and every driver
#     library that goes with it, and podman reads it. AquariusOS already
#     installs NVIDIA's container toolkit and switches on the service that
#     WRITES that file — nvidia-cdi-refresh — which regenerates it at every
#     boot and after every driver change.
#
#     That last part is why it is the right answer HERE specifically. AquariusOS
#     updates by replacing the whole operating system, driver and all. Anything
#     baked in at build time would name driver files by version and go stale the
#     first time the OS updated. A file regenerated at boot cannot.
#
# (b) distrobox's own `--nvidia` switch, which copies the host's driver files
#     into the container itself. It works, it needs no toolkit, and it is
#     distrobox's own best guess rather than NVIDIA's description of the
#     hardware. It is the fallback, used when (a) has produced no file.
#
# The device nodes themselves — /dev/dri, /dev/nvidia*, and any USB dongle —
# need no special handling: distrobox already gives its containers the host's
# devices. What (a) and (b) are for is the driver LIBRARIES, which are not
# devices and do not come across on their own.
# ==============================================================================
step 1 "Checking your graphics card"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " Looking at your graphics card"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"

GPU_FLAGS=""       # goes into --additional-flags
DISTROBOX_NVIDIA="" # the --nvidia switch, or empty
GPU_METHOD="none"

# have_nvidia, find_cdi_spec and nvidia_driver_version are defined once, near
# the top, so that this and `--gpu-summary` cannot ever disagree about what is
# in the machine.
if have_nvidia; then
    NV_VERSION="$(nvidia_driver_version)"
    echo "NVIDIA graphics card found${NV_VERSION:+, driver ${NV_VERSION}}."

    if CDI_SPEC="$(find_cdi_spec)"; then
        echo "  Using NVIDIA's own container description: ${CDI_SPEC}"
        GPU_FLAGS="--device nvidia.com/gpu=all"
        GPU_METHOD="cdi"
    else
        echo "  NVIDIA's container description has not been written on this machine."
        echo "  Falling back to distrobox's own way of sharing the driver."
        echo "  (If Resolve later says its GPU mode is unsupported, run:"
        echo "     sudo nvidia-ctk cdi generate --output=/etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml"
        echo "   then remove and re-install with:  aq resolve remove )"
        DISTROBOX_NVIDIA="--nvidia"
        GPU_METHOD="distrobox-nvidia"
    fi

    # Resolve 21 wants a 580-series driver or newer. Older ones start, and then
    # behave oddly in ways that look like Resolve being broken.
    if [ -n "${NV_VERSION}" ]; then
        NV_MAJOR="${NV_VERSION%%.*}"
        if [ "${NV_MAJOR}" -lt 580 ] 2> /dev/null; then
            echo ""
            echo "  ⚠️  Your driver is ${NV_VERSION}. DaVinci Resolve 21 expects 580 or"
            echo "      newer. Setup will carry on, but if Resolve behaves strangely"
            echo "      this is the first thing to suspect. AquariusOS updates the"
            echo "      driver with the operating system:   sudo bootc upgrade"
        fi
    fi

elif [ -e /dev/dri ]; then
    echo "An AMD or Intel graphics card was found — no NVIDIA driver here."
    echo ""
    echo "  ⚠️  BE AWARE, AND THIS IS US BEING STRAIGHT WITH YOU:"
    echo "      DaVinci Resolve on AMD needs AMD's ROCm compute libraries, which"
    echo "      are a ~24 GB install and are NOT set up in this container yet."
    echo "      Resolve will install and start, and will then most likely say"
    echo "      'GPU processing mode is unsupported'."
    echo ""
    echo "      Intel graphics are not supported by Resolve on Linux at all."
    echo ""
    echo "      Blackmagic's only supported Linux graphics is NVIDIA, and that"
    echo "      is Blackmagic's decision, not AquariusOS's. Getting AMD working"
    echo "      properly is on the plan; it is not done."
    echo ""
    GPU_METHOD="none"
else
    echo "No graphics card could be found at all. That is very unusual."
    echo "Setup can continue, but Resolve will not be able to do much."
fi

# The device nodes, named explicitly. distrobox already shares the host's
# devices with its containers, so this is belt and braces — but it makes the
# intention visible in the container's own settings, which is where somebody
# debugging this in a year will look.
if [ -e /dev/dri ]; then
    GPU_FLAGS="${GPU_FLAGS}${GPU_FLAGS:+ }--device /dev/dri"
fi

echo ""

# ==============================================================================
# 3. Find Blackmagic's download
# ==============================================================================
# This section is longer than it looks like it should be, and every line is
# there because of something that happened to a real person on 2026-08-28, on
# the first AquariusOS line — the one that was restarted from scratch on
# 2026-09-02 and is now frozen.
#
# (That old line's name is deliberately not written here. The image build
# refuses to publish an image with the previous base distribution's name
# anywhere in it, which is a rule worth keeping even when the mention is only a
# comment in a script: it is the check that catches a leftover nobody meant to
# ship. The history is in docs/base-distro-reassessment-2026-09.md.)
#
# What happened:
#
#   * The old flow opened Blackmagic's page and then said "installer not found"
#     a second later. Of course it was not found — the page had only just
#     opened. Anything that opens a download page must WAIT for the download.
#   * Blackmagic hand out a .zip. He unzipped it, which made a FOLDER inside
#     Downloads with the .run inside THAT. Looking only at the top of Downloads
#     misses it.
#   * Told there was no installer, he ran the .run himself on the real system,
#     where it stopped on a missing 'zlib'. That is a dead end and it was our
#     fault for leading him there. The warning is now printed before the
#     download starts, and again while waiting.
# ==============================================================================

step 2 "Finding your download"
mkdir -p "${DOWNLOADS}" 2> /dev/null || true

# find_one and find_installer are defined once, near the top, so that this and
# `--find-installer` cannot ever pick different files.

if [ -n "${INPUT}" ]; then
    if [ ! -e "${INPUT}" ]; then
        fail "There is no file at ${INPUT}. Choose the file you downloaded from Blackmagic and try again."
        echo "There is no file at ${INPUT}." >&2
        echo "Check the path, or run 'aq resolve install' with no file name and" >&2
        echo "let it find the download for you." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    INSTALLER="${INPUT}"
else
    INSTALLER="$(find_installer || true)"
fi

if [ -z "${INSTALLER}" ]; then
    echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
    echo " Now download DaVinci Resolve"
    echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
    echo "Opening Blackmagic's download page in your browser."
    echo ""
    echo "  * Choose DaVinci Resolve for LINUX. The free version is fine; if"
    echo "    you have bought Studio, that works here too and is the one worth"
    echo "    having on Linux — see the notes at the end."
    echo "  * They ask for a name and an email address."
    echo "  * Save the file in your Downloads folder."
    echo "  * Then LEAVE IT ALONE. Do not open it and do not run it."
    echo ""
    echo "  ⚠️  IF YOU RUN BLACKMAGIC'S INSTALLER YOURSELF it will stop with a"
    echo "      complaint about missing packages such as 'zlib'. Nothing is"
    echo "      broken when that happens — the installer simply has to be run"
    echo "      inside the container this script builds, which is the whole"
    echo "      point of this script."
    echo ""
    xdg-open "https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve" > /dev/null 2>&1 &

    # Re-name step 2 while we sit here. The window shows whatever this says, so
    # somebody who clicked Install with nothing downloaded reads "Waiting for
    # your download" rather than watching "Finding your download" spin for an
    # hour and assuming it has hung.
    step 2 "Waiting for your download from Blackmagic"
    echo "Waiting for the download to appear in ${DOWNLOADS}..."
    echo "(Ctrl+C to stop. Nothing will be left broken.)"
    echo ""
    ticks=0
    while :; do
        if INSTALLER="$(find_installer)"; then
            break
        fi
        sleep 3
        ticks=$((ticks + 1))
        if [ $((ticks % 10)) -eq 0 ]; then
            echo "  still waiting — nothing in ${DOWNLOADS} yet ($((ticks * 3 / 60)) min)"
        fi
    done
fi

echo ""
echo "Found: ${INSTALLER}"

# The container can only see files inside your home folder, because that is the
# one folder it shares. A download parked on an external drive has to be copied
# in first. Better to say so than to fail three minutes later with a path error.
case "${INSTALLER}" in
    "${HOME}"/*) ;;
    *)
        echo ""
        echo "That file is outside your home folder, and the container can only"
        echo "see files inside it. Copying it into ${DOWNLOADS} first..."
        if cp -f "${INSTALLER}" "${DOWNLOADS}/"; then
            INSTALLER="${DOWNLOADS}/$(basename "${INSTALLER}")"
            echo "Copied to ${INSTALLER}"
        else
            fail "That download is outside your home folder and could not be copied into ${DOWNLOADS}. Move it there yourself and start again."
            echo "" >&2
            echo "The copy failed. Move the download into your Downloads folder" >&2
            echo "by hand and run this again." >&2
            exit 1
        fi
        ;;
esac

# ==============================================================================
# 4. Build the container
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " Setting up the environment Resolve runs in"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one place there is a real number to report
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# podman says how many megabytes of the gigabyte have arrived. This runs its
# output through awk, which passes every line straight through to the person's
# log untouched, and separately turns any "123.4MiB / 1.1GiB" it recognises into
# a PERCENT line on the progress channel.
#
# ⚠️ IT IS ALLOWED TO RECOGNISE NOTHING. podman words its progress differently
# depending on whether it thinks it is talking to a terminal, and that wording
# is not a promise anybody made to us. If no line matches, no PERCENT is sent,
# the window shows a bar with no end, and the download works exactly as well.
# Nothing here may ever be load-bearing.
pull_with_progress() {
    local rc
    if [ "${PROGRESS_OPEN}" != "1" ]; then
        podman pull "${IMAGE}"
        return $?
    fi
    # awk inherits descriptor 9 and writes the PERCENT lines there directly.
    podman pull "${IMAGE}" 2>&1 | awk -v out="/dev/fd/9" '
        function tosize(x,   v) {
            gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t]+$/, "", x)
            v = x + 0
            if (x ~ /[kK]i?B/) return v * 1024
            if (x ~ /Mi?B/)    return v * 1024 * 1024
            if (x ~ /Gi?B/)    return v * 1024 * 1024 * 1024
            return v
        }
        {
            print
            fflush()
            if (match($0, /[0-9.]+ *[kKMG]?i?B *\/ *[0-9.]+ *[kKMG]?i?B/)) {
                s = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH)
                split(s, a, "/")
                cur = tosize(a[1])
                tot = tosize(a[2])
                if (tot > 0) {
                    p = int(cur * 100 / tot)
                    if (p >= 0 && p <= 100 && p != last) {
                        print "PERCENT " p > out
                        fflush(out)
                        last = p
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    '
    rc="${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
    return "${rc}"
}

if podman container exists "${CONTAINER}" 2> /dev/null; then
    step 3 "Downloading the environment Resolve runs in"
    if [ "${MODE}" = "update" ]; then
        echo "Reusing the existing '${CONTAINER}' container."
    else
        echo "A container called '${CONTAINER}' already exists — reusing it."
        echo "(To start completely fresh:  aq resolve remove  then run this again.)"
    fi
    pct 100
else
    step 3 "Downloading the environment Resolve runs in"
    echo "Downloading ${IMAGE}"
    echo "This is about a gigabyte and happens once."
    echo ""
    if ! pull_with_progress; then
        fail "The environment Resolve runs in could not be downloaded. The usual cause is no internet connection — try again when you are back online."
        echo "" >&2
        echo "The download failed." >&2
        echo "" >&2
        echo "The usual cause is no internet connection. Try again when you are" >&2
        echo "back online:   aq resolve install" >&2
        echo "" >&2
        echo "If the message above says 'manifest unknown', it means this exact" >&2
        echo "runtime has not been published yet. That happens when a machine is" >&2
        echo "running a test build of AquariusOS from a branch. The fix is to name" >&2
        echo "the branch's own runtime — see the note at the top of" >&2
        echo "docs/restart/resolve.md — and it does not affect released versions." >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    echo ""
    step 4 "Setting that environment up"
    echo "Creating the container..."
    # --yes            do not ask; the questions were asked above
    # --nvidia         only when NVIDIA's own description was not available
    # --additional-flags  passed straight to podman
    #
    # NOT --init. That switch runs a service manager inside the container and,
    # in distrobox's own words, disables host process integration. Resolve is
    # one program, not a system; it needs no service manager, and losing the
    # process integration would cost us the thing that makes the container
    # invisible to the user.
    #
    # NOT --home either. Sharing your real home folder is the default and it is
    # what we want: Resolve reads and writes your ordinary Videos and Projects
    # folders, so nothing has to be moved in and out.
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086  # DISTROBOX_NVIDIA is deliberately word-split
    if ! distrobox create --yes \
        --name "${CONTAINER}" \
        --image "${IMAGE}" \
        ${DISTROBOX_NVIDIA} \
        ${GPU_FLAGS:+--additional-flags "${GPU_FLAGS}"}; then
        fail "The container could not be created. Nothing on your computer was changed. Try again, or start from a clean slate with: aq resolve remove"
        echo "" >&2
        echo "The container could not be created." >&2
        echo "Nothing on your actual system was changed." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
fi

echo ""
# Said again here because the container may already have existed, in which case
# the branch above was skipped entirely and this is where step 4 really begins.
step 4 "Setting that environment up"
echo "Starting it for the first time (this takes a moment)..."
if ! distrobox enter --name "${CONTAINER}" -- true; then
    fail "The container was built but would not start. Clear it away with 'aq resolve remove' and try again."
    echo "" >&2
    echo "The container was created but would not start." >&2
    echo "Try:  aq resolve remove   and then run this again." >&2
    exit 1
fi

# ==============================================================================
# 5. Install Resolve inside it
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
step 5 "Installing DaVinci Resolve"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " Installing DaVinci Resolve"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo "This is the long part. Several minutes, and it is quiet for most of"
echo "them. Leave it alone."
echo ""

if ! distrobox enter --name "${CONTAINER}" -- \
    aquarius-resolve-setup "${INSTALLER}"; then
    fail "Blackmagic's installer did not finish. Nothing on your computer was changed — it all happened inside the container, so it is safe to try again."
    echo "" >&2
    echo "The install did not finish." >&2
    echo "Nothing on your actual system was changed — it all happened inside" >&2
    echo "the container." >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "You can safely try again:   aq resolve install" >&2
    echo "To start from a clean container:   aq resolve remove" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# ==============================================================================
# 6. Make the menu entries go through our launcher
# ==============================================================================
# The step above wrote menu entries into ~/.local/share/applications that step
# into the container and run Resolve. They work as they are. We point them at
# our own front door instead, which does the same thing plus the handful of
# things a container cannot know about the desktop around it — chiefly what your
# mouse pointer is supposed to look like. See aquarius-resolve-launch's header.
#
# It is written to be safe when it does not match: if a line is not the shape we
# expect, the file is left exactly as it was and we say so. A slightly wrong
# cursor is a far better outcome than a launcher that does not launch.
echo ""
step 6 "Adding it to your apps"
echo "Tidying up the app-menu entries..."

# distrobox-export's own Exec line looks like:
#
#     Exec=/usr/local/bin/distrobox-enter -n aquarius-resolve -e /opt/resolve/bin/resolve %u
#
# Everything after `-e` has to survive untouched, because it carries the file
# arguments (`%u`) that make "open this project with Resolve" work. So the
# replacement stops at `-e` and leaves the rest alone.
#
# `-e` OR `--`: distrobox's documented examples use `-e` for export and `--`
# for `enter`, and the exported file has used both over the years. Matching
# either costs nothing and saves a silent no-op.
APPS_DIR="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-${HOME}/.local/share}/applications"
mkdir -p "${APPS_DIR}" 2> /dev/null || true
patched=0
skipped=0
while IFS= read -r entry; do
    [ -n "${entry}" ] || continue
    # Already ours from an earlier run — nothing to do.
    grep -q "aquarius-resolve-launch" "${entry}" && continue
    if grep -qE "^Exec=.*distrobox-enter .*-n +${CONTAINER}( |$)" "${entry}"; then
        # `#` as the delimiter, because the pattern below contains a `|`.
        sed -i -E "s#^Exec=.*-n +${CONTAINER} +(-e|--) +#Exec=/usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-launch #" "${entry}"
        grep -q "aquarius-resolve-launch" "${entry}" \
            && patched=$((patched + 1)) \
            || skipped=$((skipped + 1))
    else
        skipped=$((skipped + 1))
    fi
done < <(find "${APPS_DIR}" -maxdepth 1 -type f \
    \( -iname '*resolve*.desktop' \
    -o -iname '*davinci*.desktop' \
    -o -iname '*blackmagic*.desktop' \) 2> /dev/null | sort)

[ "${patched}" -gt 0 ] && echo "  ${patched} entries now go through the AquariusOS launcher."
if [ "${skipped}" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  ${skipped} entries were left alone — they did not look the way we expected."
    echo "  Resolve will still start; the mouse pointer inside it may look wrong."
fi

update-desktop-database "${APPS_DIR}" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# ==============================================================================
# 7. Write down what was done
# ==============================================================================
# So that `aq resolve status` can answer "what am I actually running?" without
# guessing, and so that a support conversation has facts in it.
mkdir -p "${RECORD_DIR}" 2> /dev/null || true
DIGEST="$(podman image inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' "${IMAGE}" 2> /dev/null || echo 'unknown')"
{
    echo "# Written by aquarius-resolve-install. Read by 'aq resolve status'."
    echo "AQ_RESOLVE_INSTALLED_AT=$(date -Is)"
    echo "AQ_RESOLVE_IMAGE=${IMAGE}"
    echo "AQ_RESOLVE_DIGEST=${DIGEST}"
    echo "AQ_RESOLVE_GPU_METHOD=${GPU_METHOD}"
    echo "AQ_RESOLVE_SOURCE=$(basename "${INSTALLER}")"
} > "${RECORD}"

# ==============================================================================
# 8. Done
# ==============================================================================
progress "DONE"
echo ""
echo "=================================================================="
echo " Done. 'DaVinci Resolve' is now in your apps."
echo "=================================================================="
echo ""
echo "You can delete the file you downloaded — Resolve is installed now."
echo ""
echo "THE FIRST TIME YOU OPEN IT"
echo ""
echo "  * It opens as an X11 program through XWayland. That is normal and it"
echo "    is the only way Resolve runs on any Linux — it has no Wayland"
echo "    version. You will not notice."
echo ""
echo "  * On a big 4K screen the interface starts small. Fix it INSIDE"
echo "    Resolve: DaVinci Resolve menu → Preferences → User → UI Settings →"
echo "    'Interface Scale'. Set it and restart Resolve. Do not use the"
echo "    operating system's display scale for this; Resolve ignores it."
echo ""
echo "  * Check it found the graphics card: Preferences → System → Memory and"
echo "    GPU. GPU processing mode should say CUDA, and your card should be"
echo "    listed. If it says the mode is unsupported, see:"
echo "        aq resolve status"
echo ""
echo "TWO THINGS ABOUT RESOLVE ON LINUX THAT ARE NOT OUR DOING"
echo ""
echo "  * The FREE version cannot open ordinary phone and camera MP4 files at"
echo "    all on Linux — no H.264 or H.265. That is Blackmagic's licensing."
echo "  * Even Resolve STUDIO opens MP4s with SILENT AUDIO on Linux, because"
echo "    it has no AAC decoder on this platform."
echo ""
echo "  AquariusOS fixes both from the outside. Right-click your footage and"
echo "  choose 'Make Editor-Ready', or run:   aq-ingest --help"
echo "  It rewraps the audio and makes proxies, and Resolve then imports the"
echo "  files with picture and sound. No other operating system does this."
echo ""
echo "More: aq resolve --help,  and docs/restart/resolve.md"
echo ""
